var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"interface/#Implementing-a-cache","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"The containers of Cached, LRU and ClockCache, are subtypes of Cached.AbstractCache. They share all storage, locking and size accounting, and differ only in their eviction policy. A new container implements a policy in four methods, and can then be used anywhere the built-in ones are, e.g. as GlobalCache{MyCache}().","title":"Implementing a cache"},{"category":"section","location":"interface/#What-the-generic-code-guarantees","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"Thread safety. Every operation takes the cache's lock (a ReentrantLock), and every policy method is called with it held. get! releases the lock while it computes a missing value, so computations run in parallel and may recurse into the same cache. If two tasks compute the same key, the first to finish stores it; an exception stores nothing.\nKeys that do not box. Keys are stored as Key{Any}, with their hash, and looked up with a concretely typed Key{K} probe. A hit does not allocate, also in a C{Any, Any} cache like those of @cached functions, and eviction never re-hashes a key. Keys of different types are different entries, even when they are isequal.\nSize accounting. Each entry has a size: 1, or by(value) when the cache was made with a by function. Inserting evicts, through the policy, until the new entry fits in maxsize. A value larger than maxsize is returned but not stored.\nSlots. Entries live in numbered slots, reused once freed. The policy only sees slot numbers; the keys, values and sizes are in c.slots (a Cached.Slots), which it may read, e.g. c.slots.sizes[i] for a size-aware policy.\n\nAll of these methods are provided for any subtype: get!, get, getindex, setindex!, haskey, delete!, empty!, length, isempty, iteration (over a snapshot taken under the lock, in unspecified order), resize!, Cached.cache_stats and show, besides everything an AbstractDict derives from these.","title":"What the generic code guarantees"},{"category":"section","location":"interface/#The-policy-interface","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"A subtype C{K, V} <: Cached.AbstractCache{K, V}\n\nhas a field slots::Cached.Slots{V};\nhas a constructor C{K, V}(; maxsize, by), which is how GlobalCache and TaskLocalCache create it;\ntracks its occupied slots through these four methods:\n\nEvery slot passes through admit!, any number of touch! calls, and then forget!, either from an eviction (after victim chose it), a delete!, an overwrite or empty!.","title":"The policy interface"},{"category":"section","location":"interface/#Example:-first-in,-first-out","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"A cache that evicts the oldest entry, ignoring hits:\n\nusing Cached\n\nmutable struct FIFO{K, V} <: Cached.AbstractCache{K, V}\n    const slots::Cached.Slots{V}\n    const queue::Vector{Int} # occupied slots, oldest first\nend\n\nFIFO{K, V}(; maxsize = 10_000, by = nothing) where {K, V} =\n    FIFO{K, V}(Cached.Slots{V}(maxsize, by), Int[])\n\nCached.admit!(c::FIFO, i::Int) = push!(c.queue, i)\nCached.touch!(::FIFO, ::Int) = nothing\nCached.victim(c::FIFO) = first(c.queue)\nCached.forget!(c::FIFO, i::Int) = deleteat!(c.queue, findfirst(==(i), c.queue))\n\nc = FIFO{Int, String}(; maxsize = 2)\nc[1] = \"one\"\nc[2] = \"two\"\nget!(() -> \"uno\", c, 1) # a hit, which does not keep 1\nc[3] = \"three\"          # evicts 1, the oldest\nsort!(collect(keys(c))), Cached.cache_stats(c)\n\n# output\n\n([2, 3], (hits = 1, misses = 0, length = 2, currentsize = 2, maxsize = 2, by = nothing))\n\nforget! takes linear time here, which is fine for evictions (the victim is first in the queue) but not for frequent deletions; LRU keeps a doubly linked list in prev/next vectors instead. The test suite runs this FIFO through the same tests as the built-in caches.","title":"Example: first in, first out"},{"category":"section","location":"interface/#Other-locking-schemes","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"The generic methods take the lock on every lookup. A container that needs a different scheme, such as lock-free hits for a CLOCK policy, can still use Slots and the policy methods for its bookkeeping, and define its own get!, get, getindex and haskey for its type.","title":"Other locking schemes"},{"category":"type","location":"interface/#Cached.Slots","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"Cached.Slots{V}(maxsize::Integer, by)\n\nThe storage of an AbstractCache: the entries in numbered slots, the key index, the size accounting, the statistics and the lock. Slots are numbered from 1 and reused once freed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.Slots"},{"category":"function","location":"interface/#Cached.admit!","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"Cached.admit!(c::AbstractCache, i::Int)\n\nStart tracking the entry just stored in slot i. A slot that was never used before is one past the highest slot seen so far: grow the per-slot metadata. Called with the lock held.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.admit!"},{"category":"function","location":"interface/#Cached.touch!","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"Cached.touch!(c::AbstractCache, i::Int)\n\nRecord a hit on the occupied slot i. Called with the lock held, on every hit: keep it cheap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.touch!"},{"category":"function","location":"interface/#Cached.victim","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"Cached.victim(c::AbstractCache) -> Int\n\nChoose the occupied slot to evict; c is not empty. The slot is then passed to forget!. Called with the lock held.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.victim"},{"category":"function","location":"interface/#Cached.forget!","page":"Implementing a cache","text":"Cached.forget!(c::AbstractCache, i::Int)\n\nStop tracking slot i, whose entry is evicted, deleted or overwritten. The slot is freed afterwards, and may later be passed to admit! again. Called with the lock held.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.forget!"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Cache-containers","page":"Reference","text":"The interface for new containers (Cached.Slots, Cached.admit!, Cached.touch!, Cached.victim, Cached.forget!) is described in Implementing a cache.","title":"Cache containers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Caching-functions","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Caching functions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Strategies","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Managing-caches","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Managing caches"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Hooks-and-timing","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Hooks and timing"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.AbstractCache","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractCache{K, V} <: AbstractDict{K, V}\n\nSupertype of the size-bounded, thread-safe cache containers.\n\nEvery cache has a maxsize limit in the same units as its size measure: entry count by default, or bytes (any unit) when constructed with a by function that measures values. Lookups through get! never hold the lock while computing a missing value, so cached functions may recurse into the same cache, and an exception leaves the cache unchanged.\n\nKeys of different types never match, even when they are isequal: in a cache with an abstract key type, 1 and 1.0 are different entries. Lookups with a key that is not of type K convert it to K first.\n\nA subtype is an eviction policy. It keeps its entries in a field slots::Cached.Slots{V}, has a constructor C{K, V}(; maxsize, by), and tracks the entries by slot number through Cached.admit!, Cached.touch!, Cached.victim and Cached.forget!; see Implementing a cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.AbstractCache"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.LRU","page":"Reference","text":"LRU{K, V}(; maxsize::Integer = 10_000, by = nothing)\n\nLeast-recently-used cache, with the recency list stored as integer prev/next vectors instead of heap-allocated nodes. Evicts exactly the least recently used entry.\n\nmaxsize limits the number of entries, or, when by is given, the sum of by(value) over all entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.LRU"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.ClockCache","page":"Reference","text":"ClockCache{K, V}(; maxsize::Integer = 10_000, by = nothing)\n\nCache with CLOCK (second-chance) eviction, an approximation of LRU. A hit only sets the entry's reference bit; on eviction a hand sweeps the slots, clearing set bits and evicting the first entry whose bit is already clear. New entries start with a clear bit, so entries that are never hit again are evicted first.\n\nmaxsize limits the number of entries, or, when by is given, the sum of by(value) over all entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.ClockCache"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Base.resize!-Tuple{Cached.AbstractCache}","page":"Reference","text":"resize!(c::AbstractCache; maxsize::Integer)\n\nChange the size limit of c, evicting entries until it fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.resize!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.cache_stats","page":"Reference","text":"cache_stats(c::AbstractCache) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn (; hits, misses, length, currentsize, maxsize, by) for c, where by is the size measure (nothing for counting entries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.cache_stats"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/#Cached.@cached","page":"Reference","text":"@cached function f(args...; kwargs...) ... end\n@cached f(args...; kwargs...) = ...\n\nMemoize a method. The body becomes a method of Cached.implementation, and f keeps its signature (including default values and keyword arguments) but looks up the result in a cache selected by CacheStyle(f, args...).\n\nThe cache key is the tuple of positional arguments, followed by a NamedTuple of the keyword arguments if there are any. Results are stored in typed caches, one per key type: the value type is the return type annotation if present (it may depend on where parameters), and the inferred return type otherwise (or Any if inference does not give a concrete type).\n\nWorks for any method definition: qualified names (function Base.f(...)), operators and callable objects ((x::Foo)(args...)). Use uncached to bypass the cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.@cached"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.uncached","page":"Reference","text":"uncached(f, args...; kwargs...)\n\nCall the @cached function f without consulting or filling any cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.uncached"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.implementation","page":"Reference","text":"Cached.implementation(f, args...; kwargs...)\n\nThe uncached body of a @cached function f. The macro moves the body of each cached method here, dispatching on typeof(f); call it through uncached.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.implementation"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.CacheStyle","page":"Reference","text":"CacheStyle\n\nSupertype of the caching strategies used by @cached functions.\n\nThe strategy for a call f(args...) is chosen by CacheStyle(f, args...), which defaults to GlobalCache(). Specialize it to change the strategy per function or argument type:\n\nCached.CacheStyle(::typeof(f), x::SmallKey) = NoCache()\nCached.CacheStyle(::typeof(f), x::ThreadedKey) = TaskLocalCache{LRU}()\n\nSee also NoCache, GlobalCache, TaskLocalCache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.CacheStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.NoCache","page":"Reference","text":"NoCache()\n\nStrategy that calls the implementation every time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.NoCache"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.GlobalCache","page":"Reference","text":"GlobalCache{C}()\nGlobalCache()\n\nStrategy that stores results in one process-wide cache per function, of container type C <: AbstractCache. Without C, the container is the one set by the container preference (ClockCache by default), so that a CacheStyle method can return the default strategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.GlobalCache"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.GlobalLRUCache","page":"Reference","text":"GlobalLRUCache()\n\nAlias for GlobalCache{LRU}().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.GlobalLRUCache"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#Cached.TaskLocalCache","page":"Reference","text":"TaskLocalCache{C}()\nTaskLocalCache()\n\nStrategy that stores results in caches local to the current task, of container type C. C is either an AbstractCache or an AbstractDict type such as Dict; if it is not already concrete, it is completed to C{K, V}. Without C, the container is the one set by the container preference. Task-local caches need no locking but are not shared, not bounded unless C is, and are not visible to cache_info.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.TaskLocalCache"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.cache_info","page":"Reference","text":"cache_info() -> Vector{Pair{Any, AbstractCache}}\ncache_info(f) -> Vector{Pair{Any, AbstractCache}}\ncache_info(m::Module) -> Vector{Pair{Any, AbstractCache}}\n\nThe global caches, those of f, or those of the functions owned by the module m or its submodules, as f => cache pairs. A function has one cache, holding all its signatures (or one per container type, if its CacheStyle selects several), and is owned by the module that defines it, parentmodule(typeof(f)), wherever its @cached methods are written. The caches are live: they show their size and hit statistics, and can be inspected, emptied or resized directly. Task-local caches are not included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.cache_info"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.empty_caches!","page":"Reference","text":"empty_caches!()\nempty_caches!(f)\nempty_caches!(m::Module)\n\nEmpty every global cache, those of f, or those of the functions owned by the module m or its submodules (see cache_info). Statistics are kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.empty_caches!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.set_cache_size!","page":"Reference","text":"set_cache_size!(f, maxsize::Integer; by = nothing)\n\nSet the size limit of the global cache of f, overriding the preferences (see the configuration docs). The limit applies to the function as a whole, all signatures together. Without by, maxsize counts entries; otherwise it bounds the sum of by(value) over the entries, e.g. with by = Cached.cachesize for bytes. Changing by discards the cache of f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.set_cache_size!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.set_cache_preferences!","page":"Reference","text":"set_cache_preferences!(; settings...)\nset_cache_preferences!(package::Module; settings...)\nset_cache_preferences!(f; settings...)\n\nStore default cache settings in LocalPreferences.toml: globally (section [Cached]), for the functions of package ([<package>.Cached]), or for the function f ([<owner>.Cached.<f>], where <owner> is the package that defines f). Settings are maxsize, measure (\"count\" or \"bytes\"), and, globally only, container (\"ClockCache\" or \"LRU\"). A value of nothing removes the setting.\n\nSettings apply to functions whose first cache is created afterwards, so in practice after a restart; changing container recompiles Cached. See the configuration docs for how settings combine.\n\nset_cache_preferences!(; maxsize = 100_000)\nset_cache_preferences!(TensorKit; measure = \"bytes\", maxsize = 2^30)\nset_cache_preferences!(TensorKit.fsbraid; maxsize = 50_000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.set_cache_preferences!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.cachesize","page":"Reference","text":"Cached.cachesize(x) -> Integer\n\nSize in bytes of a cached value x, used by caches whose measure is \"bytes\". Defaults to Base.summarysize(x). Overload it for your own types when that is slow (it traverses the whole object) or inaccurate (memory shared between values is counted for each of them):\n\nCached.cachesize(t::MyTensor) = sizeof(t.data)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.cachesize"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.cache_dashboard","page":"Reference","text":"cache_dashboard(; interval = 1.0, filter = \"\")\n\nOpen an interactive terminal dashboard of the global caches: one row per function, with live hit rates and sizes, from which caches can be emptied or resized. The statistics are re-read every interval seconds, and only functions whose name contains filter are shown.\n\nThe dashboard is a package extension: load Tachikoma.jl first, with using Tachikoma. See Dashboard for the keybindings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.cache_dashboard"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.instrument_label","page":"Reference","text":"Cached.instrument_label(f, ::Val{phase}) -> String\n\nLabel of the phase of f in the sections recorded by enable_cache_timers!. Defaults to \"lookup f\" and \"compute f\", with the name of f; overload it to choose your own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.instrument_label"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.enable_cache_timers!","page":"Reference","text":"enable_cache_timers!(M::Module, timer::TimerOutput = TimerOutputs.get_defaulttimer())\n\nRecord the time spent in the cached functions owned by the package M, or any of its submodules, in timer: a section per function and phase, the lookup (hit or miss) with the computation of a miss nested in it, labelled by Cached.instrument_label. This includes methods of these functions cached in other packages. Modules outside packages, such as those defined in the REPL, count separately. Enabling M again replaces its timer; disable_cache_timers! stops.\n\nEnabling and disabling define and delete a method of an internal hook, so they recompile the callers of the functions of M, and only take effect for code that starts afterwards (from the next top-level statement on, or through invokelatest). They cannot be used during precompilation.\n\nThis is a package extension: load TimerOutputs.jl first, with using TimerOutputs. See Timing for the details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.enable_cache_timers!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#Cached.disable_cache_timers!","page":"Reference","text":"disable_cache_timers!(M::Module)\n\nStop timing the cached functions owned by M, started by enable_cache_timers!. Their calls recompile without the timers, at no cost again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Cached.disable_cache_timers!"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Configuration","page":"Configuration","text":"Default cache settings are read with Preferences.jl from LocalPreferences.toml, next to the active project.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Settings","page":"Configuration","text":"| Key | Values | Default | Meaning | |:-|:-|:-|:-| | maxsize | integer ≥ 0 | 10000 | limit of a function's cache, all signatures together, in entries or bytes (see measure) | | measure | \"count\" or \"bytes\" | \"count\" | count entries, or measure values with Cached.cachesize | | container | \"ClockCache\" or \"LRU\" | \"ClockCache\" | container of the default CacheStyle; only in the [Cached] section |","title":"Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Where-settings-come-from","page":"Configuration","text":"Settings are looked up per function, in this order (first match wins):\n\nruntime calls: set_cache_size!;\nthe function's own section, [<Package>.Cached.<function>];\nthe section of the package that owns the function, [<Package>.Cached];\nCached's own section, [Cached];\nthe built-in defaults above.\n\nThe package that owns a function is the package of the module that defines it, parentmodule(typeof(f)). For a function extended by several packages (for example TensorKitSectors.Fsymbol), that is the package that defines the function, not the ones that add cached methods to it.\n\n# LocalPreferences.toml\n[Cached]\nmaxsize = 10000\ncontainer = \"LRU\"\n\n[TensorKit.Cached]\nmaxsize = 50000\n\n[TensorKit.Cached.fsbraid]\nmeasure = \"bytes\"\nmaxsize = 2_000_000_000\n\nUnknown keys and invalid values are ignored with a warning.","title":"Where settings come from"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#When-changes-take-effect","page":"Configuration","text":"maxsize and measure are read when a function's first cache is created, so a change applies to functions that have not been called yet in the current session, and to every function after a restart.\ncontainer is a compile-time preference, because it selects the default CacheStyle. Changing it recompiles Cached on the next start. GlobalCache() and TaskLocalCache() use this container, so CacheStyle methods that return them follow the preference.\n\nUse set_cache_preferences! to write the sections, which merges with what is already there:\n\nusing Cached\nset_cache_preferences!(; maxsize = 50_000)                 # [Cached]\nset_cache_preferences!(TensorKit; measure = \"bytes\")       # [TensorKit.Cached]\nset_cache_preferences!(TensorKit.fsbraid; maxsize = 1000)  # [TensorKit.Cached.fsbraid]\nset_cache_preferences!(TensorKit.fsbraid; maxsize = nothing) # remove a setting","title":"When changes take effect"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Measuring-sizes","page":"Configuration","text":"With measure = \"bytes\", every cached value is measured once, on insertion, by Cached.cachesize, which defaults to Base.summarysize. That traverses the whole value, which can be slow for large nested values, and counts memory shared between values once per value. Overload it for your own types:\n\nCached.cachesize(t::MyTensor) = sizeof(t.data)","title":"Measuring sizes"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Dashboard","page":"Dashboard","text":"cache_dashboard opens a terminal dashboard of the global caches, to browse them, watch their hit rates live, and empty or resize them while your program runs. It is a package extension on Tachikoma.jl, so load Tachikoma first:\n\nusing Cached, Tachikoma\ncache_dashboard()                                # refreshes every second\ncache_dashboard(; interval = 0.2, filter = \"fs\") # faster, only functions whose name contains \"fs\"\n\nWith a few example caches it looks like this (rendered while building these docs):\n\n 4 caches, 383 entries\n Name ▲               Hit rate      Size                   Kind  Activity\n Main.fib             █████67%▋░░░░ ▏░░░░░░░61/10k░░░░░░░░ LRU   ████████\n▌Main.fsymbol         █████67%▊░░░░ ▋░░░░░░276/10k░░░░░░░░ Clock ▄█▆▃▇▅▂▆\n Main.label           ████100%█████ ▏░░░░░░░6/10k░░░░░░░░░ Clock ▇█ ▁▃▄▅▇\n Main.matrix          ████100%█████ ███▊175KiB/1.0MiB░░░░░ Clock ▆▇█▁▂▃▄▄\n\n\n\n\n╭─  Main.fsymbol  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\n│Clock · 276 of 10000 entries · 1944 hits · 276 misses                                             │\n│hit rate: recent 67.3% · lifetime 87.6% · 60.0 hits/s                                             │\n│▃▇▄▂▆▄█▅▃▇▅▂▆▄▇▅▃▆▄█▆▃▇▅▂▆▄▇▅▃▇▄█▆▃▇▅▂▆                                                           │\n╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\n ↑↓ select  ⏎ resize  e empty  s sort  r reverse  / filter  g refresh  q quit\n\nEach function is a row (or one per container type, in the rare case that its CacheStyle selects several). The columns are a bar for the recent hit rate (over the last 10 refreshes, - without lookups), a bar for the size against the limit (in bytes for caches measured in bytes), the container (LRU or Clock), and a sparkline of hits per second. The name column is as wide as the longest name; narrow terminals cut names in the middle and drop columns from the right, and below 40×8 the dashboard asks for a larger terminal. The panel at the bottom shows the numbers for the selected row, including its lifetime hit rate. Task-local caches are not shown.\n\n| Key | Action | |:-|:-| | ↑ ↓, PgUp PgDn, Home End | select a row | | Enter | resize the selected cache (see below) | | e | empty the selected cache | | s / r | sort by the next column / reverse the order | | / | filter by function name (Enter applies, Esc cancels) | | g | refresh now | | q, Esc | quit |\n\nIn resize mode, ← and → halve and double the limit, [ and ] change it by 10%, Enter applies and Esc cancels; the size bar previews the new limit. Applying calls set_cache_size! with the function's current measure (entries or bytes), so the limit persists for the function.\n\nThe statistics are read with cache_info and Cached.cache_stats, which take each cache's lock only briefly, so the dashboard is safe to run while other tasks use the caches.","title":"Dashboard"},{"category":"section","location":"timing/#Timing","page":"Timing","text":"With TimerOutputs.jl loaded, enable_cache_timers! records the time spent in the cached functions of a package, in two nested sections per function:\n\nlookup f wraps the whole cache lookup, hit or miss;\ncompute f wraps the call of the implementation: nested in lookup f on a miss, and on its own under NoCache.\n\nusing Cached, TimerOutputs\n\n@cached fsymbol(a, b, c) = a + b * c\n@cached matrix(n::Int)::Matrix{Float64} = zeros(n, n)\n\nto = TimerOutput()\nenable_cache_timers!(@__MODULE__, to)   # the timer defaults to TimerOutputs' global one\nfor i in 1:200\n    fsymbol(1 + i % 6, 1 + (7i) % 6, 1 + (13i) % 6)\n    matrix(1 + i % 10)\nend\ndisable_cache_timers!(@__MODULE__)\nto\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\u001b[1m            \u001b[0m\u001b[1m        \u001b[0m\u001b[1m               Time               \u001b[0m\u001b[1m  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m       Allocations    \u001b[0m ⋯\n                     ────────────────────────────────    ───────────────────────\n Tot / % measured:             1.07s / 3.9%                   76.9MiB / 0.8%   ⋯\n ──────────────────  ────────────────────────────────    ───────────────────────\n Section     ncalls    time    %tot     avg                alloc    %tot       ⋯\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n lookup ma…     200  27.3ms   65.9%   137μs \u001b[38;5;160m █████▎   \u001b[0m    425KiB   68.8%  2.12 ⋯\n └─ comput…      10  2.04μs    0.0%   204ns \u001b[38;5;25m          \u001b[0m   3.86KiB    0.6%     3 ⋯\n lookup fs…     200  14.1ms   34.1%  70.6μs  ██▊          193KiB   31.2%     9 ⋯\n └─ comput…       6   190ns    0.0%  31.7ns \u001b[38;5;25m          \u001b[0m         ∅       ∅       ⋯\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\u001b[36m                                                               2 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\n\nTiming is a debugging tool, and works like TimerOutputs' enable_debug_timings. When it is off, the default, it costs nothing: a cache hit has not even an extra branch. Enabling or disabling a package recompiles the callers of its cached functions, and takes effect from the next top-level statement on: a function that enables the timers and then calls cached functions needs invokelatest for those calls. Enabling a package again replaces its timer. Do not enable timers during precompilation.\n\nWhat counts is the package that owns the function (including its submodules), not where @cached was written; modules outside packages, such as in the REPL, count separately. For instance, TensorKit times its own cached functions and those of TensorKitSectors, including the @cached methods of TensorKitSectors.Fsymbol defined in SUNRepresentations:\n\nfunction TensorKit.enable_timers!()\n    enable_cache_timers!(TensorKit, GLOBAL_TIMER)\n    enable_cache_timers!(TensorKitSectors, GLOBAL_TIMER)\nend\n\nThe labels come from Cached.instrument_label, \"lookup f\" and \"compute f\" by default; overload it to pick your own:\n\nCached.instrument_label(::typeof(fsbraid), ::Val{:lookup}) = \"bookkeeping: cache fsbraid\"","title":"Timing"},{"category":"section","location":"#Cached.jl","page":"Home","text":"Transparent, strategy-aware memoization for Julia functions.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThis package is under active development and its API may still change.\n\nusing Cached\n\n@cached function fusion(a, b; normalize = true)\n    # expensive computation\nend\n\nfusion(1, 2)              # computed\nfusion(1, 2)              # looked up in fusion's cache; the result is still inferred\nuncached(fusion, 1, 2)    # bypasses the cache\n\n# choose the strategy per function and argument type\nCached.CacheStyle(::typeof(fusion), a::Int, b::Int) = TaskLocalCache{LRU}()\n\ncache_info(fusion)        # hit/miss statistics\ncache_info(MyPackage)     # ... of all cached functions of a module, empty_caches! likewise\nset_cache_size!(fusion, 1_000)\n\n@cached works on any method definition: positional, default, varargs and keyword arguments, where clauses, qualified names, operators and callable objects.\nEach function has one cache, whose maxsize (in entries or bytes) is the budget for all its signatures together. Calls stay type-stable, and cache hits do not allocate.\nCacheStyle selects the strategy per function and argument type: a shared GlobalCache (the default, a ClockCache), a TaskLocalCache, or NoCache.\nDefault sizes are configured per package and per function through preferences; see Configuration.\nWith Tachikoma.jl loaded, cache_dashboard opens a live terminal dashboard; see Dashboard.\nWith TimerOutputs.jl loaded, enable_cache_timers! times lookups and computations per package; see Timing.","title":"Cached.jl"}]
}
